1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. c5 e5 { The pawn on c5 no longer attacks d5, so Black has time to strike at d4, the base of White's pawn chain. Once d4 goes, nothing protects c5. } 4. e3 ( 4. dxe5 Bxc5 { Black takes the pawn back with a developing move. The bishop on c5 aims at f2, and Black is ahead in development. } ) ( 4. Nc3 exd4 { White hasn't played e3, so only the queen can take back on d4, and there it becomes a target. } 5. Qxd4 Nd7 { The knight goes after c5. With the bishop on f8 joining in, White can't hold the pawn. } ) ( 4. Nf3 e4 { The pawn gains time on the knight and takes space in the centre. } 5. Nfd2 ( 5. Ne5 f6 { The knight on e5 has no safe square left, so Black wins it. } ) 5... b6 { Black now attacks the head of White's chain. White can't keep a pawn on c5 without giving something up. } ) ( 4. b4 a5 { Black attacks b4, the pawn holding up c5. If b4 gives way, c5 is left hanging. } ) 4... Nd7 { The knight backs up e5 and adds pressure on c5. White's centre is stuck, and Black keeps the choice between taking on d4 and pushing to e4. } *
